{"id":"rebuild-tree-from-two-walks","lc":"construct-binary-tree-from-preorder-and-inorder-traversal","title":"Rebuild a Tree From Two Walks","diff":"medium","pattern":"trees","noted":true,"tags":["binary-tree","divide-and-conquer","hash-map"],"statement":"<p>A binary tree with <b>distinct</b> values was walked twice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>preorder</code>: node, then its left subtree, then its right subtree.</li>\n<li><code>inorder</code>: left subtree, then the node, then its right subtree.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rebuild the tree from the two lists and return its root. Both lists come from the same tree, so the answer is unique.</p>\n<p>The tree is not a search tree. Your returned tree is read back in level order with <code>null</code> for a missing child.</p>","examples":[{"in":"preorder = [5, 8, 3, 1, 6, 2], inorder = [3, 8, 1, 5, 6, 2]","out":"[5, 8, 6, 3, 1, null, 2]","why":"5 comes first in preorder, so it is the root. In inorder, 3, 8, 1 sit left of 5 and 6, 2 sit right of it. Repeat inside each part: 8 has 3 and 1 on its two sides, and 6 has 2 on its right."},{"in":"preorder = [4, 9], inorder = [9, 4]","out":"[4, 9]","why":"9 comes before 4 in inorder, so it is the left child."},{"in":"preorder = [4, 9], inorder = [4, 9]","out":"[4, null, 9]","why":"Same preorder, different shape: now 9 comes after 4 in inorder, so it is the right child. Preorder alone cannot tell these two trees apart."}],"constraints":["1 ≤ preorder.length = inorder.length ≤ 100 000","-10⁹ ≤ values ≤ 10⁹, all distinct","Both lists are walks of the same tree","The tree is at most 500 levels deep"],"fn":{"name":"rebuildTree","params":[["preorder","int[]"],["inorder","int[]"]],"returns":"TreeNode"},"tests":[{"args":[[5,8,3,1,6,2],[3,8,1,5,6,2]],"expected":[5,8,6,3,1,null,2]},{"args":[[4,9],[9,4]],"expected":[4,9]},{"args":[[4,9],[4,9]],"expected":[4,null,9]},{"args":[[7],[7]],"expected":[7]},{"args":[[1,2,3],[3,2,1]],"expected":[1,2,null,3]},{"args":[[1,2,3],[1,2,3]],"expected":[1,null,2,null,3]},{"ar